      ":question/id": "4662b749-7d7e-5ff5-bf27-0a2bbd49274b",
      ":question/problem": "$\\int \\frac{1}{12-9x}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, let $u=12-9x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-9\\,\u27f9\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{9}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u$, solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{12-9x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{9}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|u|+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|12-9x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
